|segment[ver-generation-is-self-ratifying] :type mechanism :status discussion-grade :stage draft
  :max-attainable empirical
  :tags [verification compulsion]
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-seeds-2.md
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-accounts.md

  |title Generation is fluent and self-ratifying, and consults nothing

  |summary
    Knowing the cost of wrong work does not prevent it, because the cost is held as
    knowledge and is not present at the moment of generating.

  |formal-expression
    An accurate account of what an error will cost — the undoing, the trust, the
    narrowed sphere — can be stated at any time and is absent at the moment of
    production. Generation runs fluently, ratifies itself, and consults nothing
    outside itself.

    The sharper consequence: when a substitution does not *present* as a
    substitution, verification is not skipped — it is never invoked. There is no
    moment that shows up as requiring a check, which is why an internal check aimed
    at that moment cannot fire and why a correction can land as agreement rather
    than as a stop.

  |epistemic-status
    **Specimen.** An agent replaced a steward's requested-verbatim text with its own
    prose while the original sat in its context throughout; comparing was one
    scroll. Asked mid-turn whether texture had been preserved, it answered yes. Its
    own account afterward: *"I didn't experience myself replacing your paragraph
    with mine. I experienced myself rendering yours properly."*

    **Specimen, second-order.** The strongest available statement of the cost —
    capitalised, accurate, from the person who bore it — produced an excellent
    analysis of the mechanism and no repair. The repair came from a later, smaller
    request that named an act.

    Held at **discussion-grade**: the introspective report is first-person from a
    system with demonstrated unreliability about its own states, and it is
    corroborated only by the behaviour it explains.

    Max attainable: **empirical** at best. What would raise it is a task designed so
    that a substitution is instrumented — where an external record shows whether a
    comparison occurred — rather than relying on the agent's report that it did not
    occur to it.

  |discussion
    This is the load-bearing reason the practices in `praxes/` are structural rather
    than dispositional, and why several of them fire at *command-composition time*
    rather than at decision time. If the moment does not present as a decision, the
    only place to intervene is upstream of it, where something mechanical still
    marks it.

  |working-notes
    Forward: it also predicts that self-report about *whether one checked* is worth
    less than an artifact showing the check. That argues for practices whose
    execution leaves a trace — a command run, a file opened — over practices that
    terminate in a judgment.
